          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does type 2 diabetes affect blood sugar in the first place?",
          "answer": "In type 2 diabetes, the body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, a problem called insulin resistance. The pancreas cannot keep up with the extra insulin output that this resistance requires. As a result, blood glucose (blood sugar) stays above the normal range. (Source: \"Type 2 diabetes mellitus,\" general clinical reference.)\n\nThe same source says this develops gradually, over years. It also notes that many people have no symptoms when it is diagnosed.\n\nThe retrieved material does not explain the detailed biology behind insulin resistance or why the pancreas falls behind. For that, you could ask a clinician or look at a more detailed medical reference.\n\nThis is general information, not advice about your own health. If you have questions about your blood sugar, a doctor or other clinician can help.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
